+# The novelty table — opportunities, their literature, and what they look like in the game
+
+**Date:** 2026-08-18
+**Source:** eight parallel corpus sweeps (primal extraction, dual extraction, adaptive/LOD/seams, vertex placement and error metrics, GPU and parallel, incremental and dynamic, learned and frontier, adjacent-math transfer).
+**Tier:** every row is **R** — reported from literature, read by a sweep agent, not measured here. Where a row touches an existing **M** row in `FINDINGS.md`, the M row wins. Nothing below is a claim about this crate's behaviour.
+
+**Excluded by design** — already banked, do not re-propose: Sabine reverberation on the breach frame, modal analysis of dug structures, masonry statics by alternating projection, the heat-method / Closest Point Method routing decision, medial axis and weak-feature-size certificates, contour-tree maintenance, persistence-thresholded ambiguity resolution, union-find connectivity over the air sublevel set.
+
+---
+
+## Ranked candidates
+
+Ranked by *(measured defect it addresses) × (cheapness of the falsifier)*. A candidate with a cheap falsifier beats a grand one without.
+
+| # | Opportunity | Source literature | Axis | What it looks like in the game | Cheapest falsifier |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 | **Adapt Grosso's three-quadratic tunnel test to dual cells.** Solve one quadratic per pair of *opposite* faces and check whether the hyperbola intersections form an inner hexagon; where they do, split the cell's dual vertex along the tunnel axis instead of clustering to one. This is a body-saddle test that needs all three face-pairs jointly, so it sees exactly what a face decider cannot. | Grosso, `10.1111/cgf.12975` (2016); `10.1145/3095140.3095179` (2017). Both in corpus. Manson & Schaefer (`10.1111/j.1467-8659.2009.01607.x`) state plainly that MDC and IFC each fix one symptom and neither touches the underlying cell-level ambiguity. | case table + vertex placement | Nothing visible — which is the point. It is the difference between a cave wall the physics engine understands and one where a bullet occasionally passes through. | Run the test on the 26 known offending cell-pairs in `noise_cavity`. If it does not partition them from the non-offenders, the mechanism is wrong and it cost an afternoon. **M-292 already proved no two-cell sign configuration forces the defect, so a rule with strictly more context than the shared face is the only kind that can work — this is the only such rule in the corpus.** |
+| 2 | **Union-find topological safety gate on any vertex reposition.** A cheap connectivity check that vetoes a proposed vertex move if it would change the surface's topology. | Manson & Schaefer §4. | error metric | Lets the mesher snap vertices onto the true surface for sharper silhouettes *without* the risk that snapping welds two sheets together. | Apply it to an existing snapping pass and count vetoes. If zero on all eight reference fields, it is inert here and costs nothing to drop. |
+| 3 | **Randomised per-cell tetrahedral split.** Alternate the tet diagonal by a hash of the cell's integer coordinates, cancelling the directional bias a fixed 5-tet or 6-tet decomposition bakes into every mesh. | Treece, Prager & Gee, *Regularised Marching Tetrahedra*, 1999. **Not in corpus; free copy located** (Cambridge CUED TR333) — see the procurement list. | case table | Kills the faint diagonal grain that marching tetrahedra leaves across flat walls. **M-309 measured MT at 0.250 mean ratio on `box_exact` against MC's 0.858** — tet diagonals cut an axis-aligned face into needles. This is the literature's named remedy for exactly that number. | Re-run `field_quality` on `box_exact` with the hash-alternated split. If mean ratio does not move off 0.250, the diagnosis is wrong. Deterministic by construction — the hash is a pure function of grid coordinates, so golden hashes stay reproducible. |
+| 4 | **Content-addressed vertex naming: name a vertex by its grid edge `(lower sample, axis)`, not by emission order.** | **No prior art exists.** A sweep over 9,425 documents plus a live provider search found no published scheme for stable identity of isosurface output elements without carrying mutable state. The persistence literature (Driscoll et al.) solves access to old versions and is stateful by construction; dynamic connectivity maintains aggregates, not buffer slots. | memory layout + edit locality | Digging sends 45× less data to the GPU per edit. Bigger destructible worlds at the same frame rate. | **R-027a is this ticket and it is an `S`.** Decompose the churn into geometric / predecessor-shift / order-only terms on the existing `edit_trace` bench. If the order-only term does not dominate, only the persistent map helps and R-027 stays stopped. |
+| 5 | **HistoPyramid compaction as a deterministic reduction tree.** Build an explicit sum-reduction pyramid over per-cell output counts, then walk it to place each vertex — no racing atomic counter anywhere. | Dyken, Ziegler, Theobalt, Seidel, `10.1111/j.1467-8659.2008.01182.x`. In corpus. | parallelisation + memory layout | GPU meshing that produces **byte-identical output to the CPU path**, so golden hashes survive the GPU. | Compare GPU and CPU output hashes on one reference field. Either they match or they do not; there is no ambiguity and no timing involved. |
+| 6 | **Exact predicates only where the sign can be wrong.** Evaluate the corner-vs-isovalue sign exactly via indirect predicates, applied selectively rather than everywhere. | Attene, `10.1016/j.cad.2020.102856`; Cherchi et al., `10.48550/arXiv.2205.14151`. In corpus. The crate already has exact `orient2d` and `incircle` from T-024a/b. | correctness | Digging a wall exactly on a voxel boundary stops producing a crack or a sliver. **M-317 measured 16,284 corners sitting at exactly zero on the bonsai volume** — this is not a hypothetical configuration, it is 3% of a real dataset. | Author a field with corner values set exactly to the isovalue (trivial), and count manifold failures before and after. |
+| 7 | **Bounded per-vertex grid deformation, set analytically.** Nudge each grid corner toward the surface by one Newton step along the gradient, bounded to a fraction of the cell diagonal, before applying the case table. | FlexiCubes, `10.48550/arXiv.2308.05371`. In corpus. **The deformation is analytic; only the split-weight is load-bearing on the optimiser** — take the first, leave the second. | vertex placement | Sharper edges on CSG cuts and machined shapes without changing algorithm or resolution. | **M-315 is the gate, and it may kill this outright**: MC is tessellation-limited on 8/8 rows, so moving *grid corners* is not the same lever as moving *surface vertices* and may still be under the ceiling. Measure Hausdorff on `box_exact` and `csg_difference` first. If it does not beat the placement ceiling, stop. |
+| 8 | **Discrete Morse decimation for topology-safe LOD.** Pair simplices into a discrete gradient vector field and collapse along it; every collapse is a combinatorial homotopy equivalence, so it cannot change χ or introduce a non-manifold edge. No floating-point decision anywhere. | Edelsbrunner/Harer/Natarajan/Pascucci, `10.1145/777792.777846`; Benedetti & Lutz, arXiv:1303.6422. In corpus. | error metric + LOD | Distant chunks simplify hard with **no pop-in artefacts, no flipped normals, no bowties** — the exact failure class M-301's bowtie fixture exists to catch. | Run quadric-error collapse against Morse collapse on the eight golden fixtures at ten decimation levels; count χ deviations and non-manifold-vertex link failures. Reuses the existing validity harness wholesale. |
+| 9 | **Directional / multi-normal distance samples for sub-voxel thin sheets.** Store several normal-oriented distance values per voxel instead of one scalar, so two nearby opposite-facing surfaces stop cancelling into one number. | Splietker & Behnke, arXiv:1908.05146. In corpus. | field representation | Thin destructible walls, ramparts and plate geometry stop being eaten by the mesher. Directly serves the CAD consumer, where thin walls are the common case. | Build a sub-cell-thickness wall field; mesh with scalar sampling versus directional sampling; count holes and collapsed triangles. No GPU needed. |
+| 10 | **Range analysis over the field for guaranteed coarse-cell traversal.** Interval arithmetic gives a certificate that a coarse cell provably contains no surface, instead of a heuristic sample-the-corners test. | Sharp & Jacobson, `10.1145/3528223.3530155`. In corpus. | traversal order | An octree or empty-cell rejection pass that **cannot** miss a thin sheet. `gyroid` is the adversary: **M-306 measured its rejected share at 16.8% against `thin_plate`'s 95.1%**, a 5.7× spread, and a coarse cell straddling a gyroid sheet is exactly where corner sampling lies. | Count cells where corner-sampling rejects and interval arithmetic does not, on `gyroid` and `thin_plate`. If zero, corner sampling was already safe on these fields and the certificate buys nothing. |
